<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transpadane Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Transpadane Republic was a sister republic of revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, north of the Po, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> conquered the Austrian Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 1796. With Milan as its capital, its new institutions replaced the old Austrian administration. In 1797 it became the core of the Cisalpine Republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transpadane-republic.jpg" alt="Transpadane Republic" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PavelD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Repubblica_Transpadana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Transpadane Republic</b> was set up in November 1796 and merged into the Cisalpine Republic in 1797, and Austria renounced Lombardy in the Treaty of Campo Formio that October.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
